# How a close proves a deploy landed, strongest form first, and the credentials the probe reads
# BY NAME from the environment.
#
# POLICY.md never holds a secret. Its `liveness` table holds the NAME of an environment variable,
# and the gate reads the value at probe time. Nothing here is ever printed in a verdict, in an
# error, or in a report.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in real values, or export these however your machine
# already handles secrets.
#
# EVERY NAME STARTS WITH `PANDORAS_`. The probe refuses to read a variable outside that prefix, so
# a policy row can only ever reach a secret minted for the probe, never a key that happens to be
# in the environment. (Configurable: `probeLiveness({ envPrefix })`.)
#
# ══ 1. THE SHA ECHO — the default proof ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The deployment answers a JSON route with the commit it was built from. The close GETs it and
# passes only when that commit is, or contains, the lane's merge commit. This is the only proof
# that cannot pass on stale bytes: an older build does not know a commit it does not contain.
# Its verdict reads "deployment identity". Use it wherever the surface can echo its own commit.
#
# POLICY.md repos table:
#   | `web` | 1 | 3 | product | 5173 | push | `sha:/api/status:release` | https://web.example.com |
# No liveness row needed, no credential, no variable. Most rows should look like this.
#
# ══ 2. THE HEADER ECHO — the same proof, read from a response header ══════════════════════════
# For a deployment that names its release in a header rather than a body (a platform's deployment
# id header, a CDN release tag, a header your app sets on purpose). The close GETs url+path, reads
# that ONE header, and passes when its value contains the merge commit (a 7+ character prefix
# counts, because platforms abbreviate). The body is never read, so a sha that appears only in the
# body counts for nothing. Same strength as the sha echo, same "deployment identity" verdict.
#
# POLICY.md repos table:
#   | `api` | 1 | 2 | product | 5174 | push+fns | `header:/api/status:X-Release` | https://api.example.com |
# A gated route takes the same `auth` shapes as section 3, named in the repo's liveness row.
#
# ══ 3. THE STRING FORM — last resort, and it says so ══════════════════════════════════════════
# A marker that must appear in a response body. It is the ONLY option when the surface cannot echo
# its commit at all (a static host with no release route or header, a page you do not control the
# headers of), and it does NOT prove that the served build is the merged commit: a cached response,
# a stale build that happens to carry the string, or an unrelated route all pass it. Its verdict
# reads "best-effort evidence" and names that gap. The three credential shapes below belong to it.
#
# ── a public URL needs nothing at all ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POLICY.md liveness table:   | `web` | https://web.example.com/ | - | - | - |
# No variable. No credential. A 200 alone passes, labelled best-effort.
#
# ── HTTP basic auth ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POLICY.md liveness table:   | `repo-a` | https://repo-a.example.com/health | `build-ok` | `basic:PANDORAS_REPO_A_PROBE_AUTH` | 8000 |
# The value is `user:password`. The gate base64-encodes it into an Authorization header. An
# already-encoded `Basic xxxx` value is passed through rather than double-encoded.
PANDORAS_REPO_A_PROBE_AUTH=probe-user:replace-me

# ── a session cookie ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POLICY.md liveness table:   | `docs` | https://docs.example.com/ | `build-ok` | `cookie:PANDORAS_DOCS_PROBE_COOKIE` | - |
# The value is the whole Cookie header, e.g. `session=abc123`.
PANDORAS_DOCS_PROBE_COOKIE=session=replace-me

# ── a custom header, for a deployment gated by a token ────────────────────────────────────────
# POLICY.md liveness table:   | `api` | https://api.example.com/status | `build-ok` | `header:X-Deploy-Token:PANDORAS_API_PROBE_TOKEN` | - |
# (This `header:` is the AUTH column: a request header the probe SENDS. Section 2's `header:` is
# the VERIFY column: a response header the probe READS. Different columns, different directions.)
PANDORAS_API_PROBE_TOKEN=replace-me

# IF A VARIABLE IS UNSET, THE GATE SKIPS. It does NOT send the request bare and grade the 401:
# that would measure the credential rather than the deployment. The skip says which variable was
# missing, and a skip is never a pass. A credentialed probe never follows a redirect, and a body
# is read to 1 MB and no further; the header echo reads no body at all.
